---
source: crates/trousseau-cli/tests/help.rs
expression: stdout
---
Copy a legacy v0.4 store into a new store.

Reads the old file (usually `~/.trousseau`) and creates a new store, following the same rules and
flags as `init`. The old file is never modified or deleted.

Usage: trousseau migrate [OPTIONS] <SOURCE>

Arguments:
  <SOURCE>
          The legacy v0.4 store file to read

Options:
      --gpg <PATH>
          The `gpg` binary to use for an `OpenPGP` legacy store

      --gnupg-home <PATH>
          An isolated `GNUPGHOME` to use when invoking `gpg`

      --new-passphrase-file <PATH>
          A file holding the new store's passphrase (only with `--passphrase`)

      --recipient <R>
          A recipient (public key) to encrypt to: `age1...`, an SSH public key, or a plugin
          recipient. Repeatable

      --recipients-file <PATH>
          A file of recipients, one per line (blank lines and `#` comments ignored)

      --passphrase
          Create a passphrase store instead of a recipients store

      --no-self
          Do not add the caller's own recipient to a recipients store

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Global options:
      --store <PATH>
          Use this store file. Wins over `--global` and over store discovery
          
          [env: TROUSSEAU_STORE=]

      --global
          Use your personal store instead of the nearest project store (the `.trousseau` file in
          this directory or a parent)

      --identity <PATH>
          An identity (private key) file to try when unlocking. Repeatable. Also tried:
          `TROUSSEAU_IDENTITY_FILE`, the config file's `identity.files`, the default identity file,
          `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` and `~/.ssh/id_rsa`

      --passphrase-file <PATH>
          Read the store's passphrase from this file instead of prompting. One trailing newline is
          stripped

      --json
          Print one JSON document on stdout instead of text. Errors become a JSON object on stderr

      --quiet
          Suppress informational messages on stderr. Errors still print

      --no-input
          Never prompt. A command that would prompt fails instead: exit 4 for a secret, exit 2 for a
          confirmation

Examples:
  Migrate into your personal store:
    trousseau migrate ~/.trousseau --global

  Migrate into a new store file of your choice:
    trousseau migrate ~/.trousseau --store ./team.trousseau

  Migrate into a passphrase store:
    trousseau migrate ~/.trousseau --global --passphrase

Notes:
  Without --global or --store, the new store is '.trousseau' in the
  current directory. Run from your home directory, that is the legacy file
  itself, and migrate stops with exit 8. Use --global or --store there.
  The legacy passphrase comes from --passphrase-file or a prompt.
  OpenPGP legacy stores need the 'gpg' binary (see --gpg).
  Keys with characters the new format does not allow are renamed, and
  every rename is printed.
